---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout class="resume">
  <hgroup>
    <h1>Carter McBride</h1>
    <p>Frontend Engineer · Saratoga Springs, Utah</p>
    <p>
      <a href="mailto:jobs@carter.works">jobs@carter.works</a> ·
      <a href="https://carter.works">carter.works</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/carterworks">github.com/carterworks</a> ·
      <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/thatcartermcbride/"
        >linkedin.com/in/thatcartermcbride</a
      >
    </p>
  </hgroup>

  <p>
    Frontend engineer since 2016, working mostly in JavaScript, TypeScript and
    the web platform. Makes web software faster for the people using it and
    easier to ship for the people building it, and likes working directly with
    the customers who depend on it.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Experience</h2>

    <article>
      <h3>Software Engineer 3, Adobe <span>Jan 2022 – Present</span></h3>
      <p>Lehi, Utah · promoted from Software Engineer 2 in 2023</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Second most senior of four engineers on Adobe’s open-source Web SDK
          (<a href="https://github.com/adobe/alloy">adobe/alloy</a>), the
          analytics and personalization library on enterprise sites. Lead most
          feature work and customer issues, and review the team’s code and
          designs; wrote about 60% of its merged pull requests in 2026.
        </li>
        <li>
          Proposed and designed a Universal JavaScript architecture that frees
          the SDK’s core from the browser; built most of it. It now also ships
          as a Node.js SDK, and runs on Cloudflare Workers in prototypes.
        </li>
        <li>
          Pushed for and led a monorepo, automated releases and deterministic
          Vitest and Playwright tests. Releasing went from a wiki checklist the
          whole team swarmed on to two clicks, with continuous betas; stable
          releases rose from about 7 a year to 12 so far in 2026.
        </li>
        <li>
          Sole engineer on the Experience Platform Debugger, a Chrome extension
          with about 80,000 users, for four of the past five years; moved it to
          Manifest V3 when Google retired V2.
        </li>
        <li>
          Spotted and fixed lag in the SDK’s Tags extension, cutting typing delay
          in its settings from 140–209&nbsp;ms to under 15&nbsp;ms, and moved its UI to
          React Spectrum 2.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h3>
        Software Developer, ICE Mortgage Technology (formerly Simplifile) <span
          >Jan 2019 – Jan 2022</span
        >
      </h3>
      <p>Provo, Utah</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Built and sped up features in Simplifile’s e-recording application,
          which sends real-estate documents to county recorders electronically.
        </li>
        <li>
          Formally mentored support technicians through their first year as
          engineers, after they moved over from a coding bootcamp.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h3>
        Software Engineering Intern, Lingotek <span>May 2018 – Dec 2018</span>
      </h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Moved pages of the main app to Vue.js for 15% faster loads, and cut
          its build time by 25%.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h3>
        Front-End Web Developer, Brigham Young University <span
          >Feb 2016 – May 2018</span
        >
      </h3>
      <p>Provo, Utah</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Built features for Learning Suite, BYU’s learning management system,
          used by thousands, with unit tests and automated setup that kept it
          reliable and onboarding quick.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Projects</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://yazzy.carter.works">Yazzy</a>: reader mode for any
        web article, in Astro.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/carterworks/substack-to-epub"
          >substack-to-epub</a
        >: saves a Substack newsletter to SQLite and formats it as an EPUB, in
        Python.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://carter.works">carter.works</a>: this site, with its
        pixel-art backdrop extracted into a self-contained web components package.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Education</h2>
    <h3>
      B.S. Computer Science, Brigham Young University <span>2015 – 2019</span>
    </h3>
    <p>
      Minor in Portuguese; fluent in Brazilian Portuguese. Third place, BYU
      Google Games 2017.
    </p>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  :global(.resume) {
    --flow-space: var(--size-sm);

    display: grid;
    gap: var(--size-md);
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: 1.6em;
  }

  h2 {
    border-block-end: 1px solid;
    font-size: 1em;
    margin-block-end: var(--size-xs);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h3 {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    font-size: 1em;
    justify-content: space-between;
  }

  h3 span {
    font-weight: normal;
  }

  article + article {
    margin-block-start: var(--size-sm);
  }

  ul {
    padding-inline-start: 1.2em;
  }


  @media print {
    @page {
      margin: 0.5in;
      size: letter;
    }

    :global(html) {
      color-scheme: light;
      font-size: 10pt;
    }

    :global(body) {
      background: none;
      color: #000;
      display: block;
    }

    :global(pixel-backdrop),
    :global(body > .stack > header) {
      display: none;
    }

    :global(.resume) {
      background: none;
      gap: var(--size-sm);
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
    }

    a {
      color: inherit;
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    a[href]::after {
      content: none;
    }
  }
</style>
